<spell-notebook "example usage">
	<header level="1">
		Example
		<header>An example shows its own markup live, under a heading and description, with the same markup as code a button shows and hides.</>
	</>
	<example-tab-bar showing="usage"/>
	<section>
		<html>
			<ui-menu class="ui-docs-section-tabs" appearance="segmented" aria-label="Sections">
				<ui-item link="#using-it">Using it</>
				<ui-item link="#keyboard">Keyboard</>
				<ui-item link="#accessibility">Accessibility</>
			</>
		</>
	</>

	<!-- USAGE:  how to use it (attributes in practice, events, framework snippets), keyboard, accessibility. -->
	<section label="Using it">
		<markdown>
			`<ui-example>` shows a piece of markup working, with the same markup as code.

			- Its children are the example, live;  their markup, as the page wrote it, is the code shown.
			- `heading` and `description` sit above it, on the page;  a backticked word in `description` is code.
			- The code button shows and hides the code, and `code` follows (`ui-opened` / `ui-closed`);
			  write `code` to open it from the start.
			- A `<template>` child is stamped out live, and its markup shown exactly as typed.
			- Any page may have one:  Spell UI's pages, the guides, an epic page.
			  A `<ui-toc>` lists each example with a `heading`.
			- On GitHub a README shows no live element:  there an example is its picture (`spell readme pictures`).
		</>
		<markdown>
			```html
			<ui-example heading="Emphasis" description="A button can show different levels of emphasis.">
			  <ui-button color="primary">Save</ui-button>
			</ui-example>
			```
		</>
		<markdown>
			```js
			document.querySelector("ui-example").addEventListener("ui-opened", () => console.log("code shown"))
			```
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Keyboard">
		<markdown>
			- Tab reaches the code button, then the example's own controls.
			- Enter or Space on the code button shows or hides the code.
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Accessibility">
		<markdown>
			- The code button is named by what a click does ("Show code", "Hide code"),
			  with `aria-expanded` and `aria-controls` naming the code pane.
			- The heading is a real heading, at `level`:  the page's outline reads it.
			- The example's own elements keep their roles:  nothing is wrapped in a landmark.
		</>
	</>
</spell-notebook>
